dialogue is: {{ [doctor] good morning, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing well, thank you.
[doctor] great. so, it's good to see you. so, i understand that you're here for your annual exam. so, how are you doing?
[patient] i'm doing well. i just started working at a bakery this summer, so that's been a big change.
[doctor] that sounds like a fun job. how's that going?
[patient] yeah, it's been good. i really like it. i just started in the summer, so it's been a little bit of an adjustment, but i'm really enjoying it.
[doctor] that's great. and how's your diabetes been?
[patient] so, my diabetes is uncontrolled. my blood sugars have been fluctuating a lot lately, so i've been trying to improve my diet. i've been removing some of the extra sweets that i was eating before.
[doctor] okay. how's that going?
[patient] it's been a little bit tough, but i'm trying to make some changes.
[doctor] okay. and have you been missing any of your medication?
[patient] no, i've been taking all of my medication.
[doctor] okay. and your blood pressure has been okay?
[patient] yeah, my blood pressure has been good.
[doctor] okay. and how's your sleep?
[patient] i've been sleeping really well. i'm able to sleep a full 8 hours.
[doctor] that's great. and have you been having any thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations?
[patient] no, i have not.
[doctor] okay. great. so, let's do a quick physical exam. i'm gon na listen to your heart and lungs and check your neck and your blood pressure. so, let's start with your heart and lungs. your heart sounds good. you have a regular rate and rhythm, and there is a 2 out of 6 systolic ejection fraction murmur, but that's not uncommon for people with hypertension. your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally, which means that your lungs sound clear. and your neck is supple without any swelling or any lumps. your blood pressure is a little bit higher than i would like to see, but it's not extremely high. your grip strength is equal bilaterally, which is good, and your neurological exam is intact. so, looking at your results, your hemoglobin a1c was elevated, which means that your diabetes is not controlled. i'm gon na continue you on your metformin twice a day, but i do want you to check your fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call them in about 2 weeks. we'll discuss further treatment recommendations when you come back in 2 months. for your high blood pressure, it looks like you're doing well. we'll continue you on your lisinopril 10 milligrams once a day. do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, that's all good.
[doctor] okay. great. do you have any other questions or concerns?
[patient] no, i think that's all.
[doctor] okay. good. all right, so i'm gon na go ahead and order some blood work and we'll get that back to you in about 2 weeks. and then we'll discuss further treatment recommendations at that time. does that sound good?
[patient] that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. and we'll get you set up for a follow-up appointment in about 2 months. do you have any other questions?
[patient] no, that's all.
[doctor] okay. good. thanks for coming in today. take care. }}<|eot_id|>
