dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, ms. thompson, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing alright, doctor. i've been doing a lot of swimming and trying to be more active.
[doctor] good. i'm glad to hear that. i see that you're here today for some joint pain. can you tell me more about that?
[patient] yes, i've been swimming a lot and it's been really good for my depression, but i've been having some knee pain.
[doctor] okay. so you're swimming a lot more and you're having some knee pain?
[patient] yes, my right knee is just really hurting.
[doctor] okay. so it hurts after you swim a couple of laps?
[patient] yeah.
[doctor] okay. and you've been swimming a lot more?
[patient] yes, i've been trying to be more active.
[doctor] okay. have you injured your knee or had any trauma to it?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any problems with your back lately?
[patient] no, my back has been doing well.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any pain radiating down your legs?
[patient] no, it's just my right knee.
[doctor] okay. and how about your depression? how are you doing with that?
[patient] it's been going really well. i've been trying to get outside a lot and that's been helping me a lot.
[doctor] okay. that's great. have you been sleeping well?
[patient] yes, i have been sleeping well.
[doctor] okay. and eating well?
[patient] yes, i have been eating well.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any concerns about hurting yourself or others?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. great. let's talk a little bit about your carpal tunnel release. how are you doing with that?
[patient] it's doing really well.
[doctor] okay. are you able to get full range of motion in your hands?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] and how is that feeling?
[patient] it feels a lot better.
[doctor] okay. great. so let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i'm gon na check your neck. your neck is supple without any thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy. your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. your heart is in regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. no extra heart sounds. let's check your joints. i'm gon na press here on your knee. that hurts?
[patient] yes, it does.
[doctor] okay. and i'm gon na check the other knee. that's okay. and i'm gon na do a straight leg raise. how about that?
[patient] it hurts.
[doctor] okay. and i'm gon na do the other one. that's okay. let's look at your knee. i'm gon na order some x-rays of your right knee. i'm gon na go ahead and review those now. your x-ray of your right knee is unremarkable. so i'm gon na go ahead and give you a diagnosis of chondromalacia of the right patella. i'm gon na prescribe you some ibuprofen 600 mg, four times a day with food. i also want you to do some straight leg raising exercises to strengthen your quadriceps. i would encourage you to participate in physical activity that's less straining on your joints, such as swimming freestyle laps, or to stop activity all together. if your pain doesn't improve in a couple of weeks, we'll follow up.
[patient] okay.
