[doctor] That's a big improvement. How about your balance? Has that improved as well?

[patient] Yes, it's a bit better, but I still have some trouble.

[doctor] Okay. And how about your bladder control?

[patient] It was a little better at first, but now I've leveled off and I still have to wear a diaper.

[doctor] Alright. How about your headaches? Do you have any headaches or pain at the shunt site or in your abdomen?

[patient] No, no headaches or pain at all.

[doctor] That's good to know. Let's talk a bit about your past medical history. You had urinary incontinence, adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, and cognitive impairment. Have you had any other illnesses or hospitalizations since your last visit?

[patient] No, none.

[doctor] Okay. Let's go through your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 124/80, your heart rate is 64, your respiratory rate is 18, your weight is 174 pounds, and your pain is 0 out of 10. How are you feeling overall?

[patient] I'm feeling okay, but I'm still having some problems.

[doctor] Alright. Let's do a quick physical exam. I'll start with your head and face. I see that the shunt site is clean, dry, and intact, and it's currently set at 2.0. How are you feeling about your shunt?

[patient] It's been okay. I haven't had any problems.

[doctor] Great. Now, let's check your neurological status. I'll test your mental status by asking you some questions. Can you spell the word "world" for me?

[patient] W-O-R-L-D.

[doctor] Excellent. Now, can you give me the dates of the month?

[patient] January 15th.

[doctor] Good. Now, I'll ask you some memory questions. Can you remember the names of the days of the week?

[patient] Yes, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday.

[doctor] That's correct. Now, I'll give you three words and ask you to repeat them back to me. The words are "dog," "cat," and "bird." Can you remember these words?

[patient] Dog, cat, bird.

[doctor] Great. Now, I'll give you a third option. You can choose one of the words that I didn't mention. Can you remember one of those words?

[patient] Yes, dog.

[doctor] Excellent. Now, let's check your gait. We'll use the Tinetti assessment tool. Can you walk for me without an assistive device?

[patient] Yes, I can walk.

[doctor] Okay. Let's start. Take a few steps.

[patient] (walks)

[doctor] How are you feeling?

[patient] A bit unsteady.

[doctor] Alright. Now, I'll check your balance. Can you stand on one foot?

[patient] (stands on one foot)

[doctor] Good. Now, I'll check your coordination. Can you touch your nose with your finger?

[patient] (tries to touch nose)

[doctor] Not quite. It's a bit slow. Now, let's check your cranial nerves. Can you follow my finger with your eyes?

[patient] Yes, I can do that.

[doctor] Excellent. Now, let's check your motor function. Can you move your arms and legs?

[patient] Yes, I can move them.

[doctor] Good. Now, let's look at the results of your CT scan from October 15th. We can see a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Munro of 4.6 cm with a 3rd ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. The radiologist didn't notice this tiny amount of fluid, but I think it's not causing any symptoms.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Based on your current symptoms and the test results, it seems like you've made some improvement with your hydrocephalus since the surgery. There is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region, but I don't think it's causing any symptoms.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] I think it's unlikely that you'll be able to drive again. Occasionally, patients surprise me by improving enough to pass a driver's safety evaluation, but I think it's unlikely in your case. However, you can always enroll in a driver's safety program without my recommendation.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] I'll leave the shunt setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing it. Let's schedule a follow-up appointment in three months.

[patient] Alright, that sounds good.

[doctor] Do you have any other questions?

[patient] No, I think that's it.

[doctor] Great. I'll see you in three months.

[patient] Thank you, doctor.

[doctor] Take care, and see you soon.

[patient] Thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
