dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, amanda, how are you?
[patient] hi, doctor. i'm doing okay.
[doctor] good to see you. how have you been?
[patient] i've been doing well. just trying to manage my blood pressure.
[doctor] alright. so i see that you're here for a follow-up. you were here last time for hypertension and alcohol use disorder. what's going on?
[patient] yes, i've been managing my blood pressure. it's been a little bit higher.
[doctor] okay. so, at your last visit on april 16th, your blood pressure was 130 over 90. and then you presented at 150 over 100. we put you on hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 milligrams once daily and lisinopril 20 milligrams once daily. and how are you doing on that?
[patient] i've been taking the medication, but my blood pressure is still a little high.
[doctor] okay. well, your cmp and cbc were essentially unremarkable. have you been checking your blood pressure?
[patient] yes, i've been checking it in the morning and evening. it's a little higher in the evening.
[doctor] okay. and what's your blood pressure been?
[patient] it's been averaging 135 to 145 over 95 to 110.
[doctor] okay. have you noticed any side effects from the lisinopril?
[patient] yes, i've had some headaches and this feeling like my ear is filled with water.
[doctor] okay. and how have you been doing with that?
[patient] i took half a dose and i didn't have the side effects.
[doctor] okay. and how about the hydrochlorothiazide?
[patient] i've been taking that.
[doctor] okay. have you noticed any side effects from that?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. do you have any other questions about your medications?
[patient] no.
[doctor] alright. so, i know you've been drinking quite a bit of alcohol. i know you said you've been drinking four to five times a week. have you thought about cutting back on that?
[patient] yeah, i've been trying.
[doctor] okay. do you think your blood pressure would improve if you stopped drinking?
[patient] yeah, i think so.
[doctor] okay. and how about naltrexone? are you interested in trying that?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay. i think that would be a good idea. we'll start you on the injectable form. we'll do it every month and we'll start it on may 17th. we'll review the side effects and risks with you. the potential benefit of alcohol cessation would be highly beneficial to you.
[patient] okay, that sounds good.
[doctor] alright. so, you'll start the naltrexone on may 17th and we'll see you back in a month. and then we'll repeat it again and hopefully your blood pressure will improve.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and quite possibly your blood pressure will improve significantly if you're able to completely stop consuming alcohol.
[patient] yeah, i think so.
[doctor] alright. i'm going to continue you on the lisinopril 10 milligrams a day and the hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 milligrams a day. and we'll see you back in the week of june 14th which will likely be the time of your second naltrexone injection. have you had your flu shot recently?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. i'll upgrade that for you and also get you scheduled for the shingrix vaccine, the second one. do you want me to go ahead and do that?
[patient] yeah, that would be good.
[doctor] okay. i'll get that taken care of. do you have any other questions or concerns?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] alright. well, call the clinic with any questions. make a follow-up appointment week of june 14th. i'll see you then.
[patient] okay, thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. take care.
[patient] you too.
[doctor] bye.
[patient] bye. }}<|eot_id|>
