dialogue is: {{ [doctor] jacqueline miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. she also has a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea. so, jacqueline, how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing well. thank you.
[doctor] great. so, how are you doing with the perioral dermatitis? i know we saw you last week, and i know you were doing okay. how are you doing now?
[patient] it's still here, but it's not as bad as it was.
[doctor] okay. so it's still here, but it's not as bad. that's good to hear. have you been using the doxycycline?
[patient] i was using it twice a day, but my pharmacist told me to reduce it to once a day since it had started to improve.
[doctor] okay. so you've been using it once a day for about a week now?
[patient] yes, for about a week now.
[doctor] okay. and how has it been doing? has it continued to improve?
[patient] it has not gotten any worse, but it has not gotten any better either.
[doctor] okay. all right. so it's sort of plateaued. okay. have you been using the sulfacetamide wash?
[patient] yes, i use it twice a day.
[doctor] and do you feel like it's drying out your skin?
[patient] no, it's not drying out my skin at all.
[doctor] okay. that's good to hear. and you're using the metrocream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash?
[patient] yes, i do.
[doctor] okay. have you been using any other lotions or products?
[patient] no, i have not.
[doctor] and you're not using any cetaphil lotion or soap, or neutrogena wipes?
[patient] no, i'm not using any of those.
[doctor] okay. so, i know you were thinking about using elidel. i know it's not covered by your insurance, but i was wondering if you ended up trying it.
[patient] no, i did not.
[doctor] okay. so, i think that's good. i was concerned about some of the side effects from the medication. so, let's go ahead and do a quick exam. so, on your face, on your bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. your fitzpatrick skin type is four to five. so, i'm gon na go ahead and prescribe you some tacrolimus cream. you can apply that once a day, and that should help with the redness and inflammation. i want you to continue using the sulfacetamide face wash once a day. and i want you to continue using the metrocream twice a day. for your eyes, i want you to gently wash your eyelids once a day with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as vanicream. and then, i want you to come back in four weeks, and we'll see how you're doing. does that sound good to you?
[patient] yes, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. all right. and if your symptoms get any worse, you should call the clinic. okay?
[patient] yes, i will.
[doctor] all right. thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
