dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you today?

[patient] I'm doing okay, thanks. I'm here for my elbow pain.

[doctor] Sure. I'm Dr. Lee. I see that you're here for right elbow pain. Can you tell me a bit more about what's going on?

[patient] Sure. The pain started four days ago and it hasn't gotten any better. I've been trying to use a lot of ibuprofen and ice, but nothing seems to work.

[doctor] Okay. Have you had any injuries recently that might have caused the pain?

[patient] No, I haven't had any injuries. But I have been using my pottery wheel a lot lately to make a bowl for my son.

[doctor] Ah, okay. How often have you been using the pottery wheel?

[patient] I've been using it every day for the past week or so.

[doctor] And how long have you been using it for?

[patient] I've been doing it for about an hour a day.

[doctor] Alright. And how would you describe the pain in your elbow?

[patient] It's mostly on the inside of my elbow, and it radiates down my arm as well.

[doctor] Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. How would you rate the pain on a scale of 1 to 10?

[patient] It's about a 7 out of 10 at its worst.

[doctor] Okay. Does the pain wake you up at night?

[patient] Yes, it does. If I roll over, it wakes me up.

[doctor] Alright. Have you tried any other treatments like ibuprofen or ice, but they didn't help?

[patient] No, ibuprofen and ice haven't worked.

[doctor] Okay. Have you had any elbow injuries before?

[patient] No, I haven't.

[doctor] Alright. Let's talk a bit about your medical history. You have a history of hypertension. How have you been managing that?

[patient] I've been checking my blood pressures regularly. They've been in the 110s to 130s.

[doctor] Okay. And are you taking your medication, lisinopril 10 mg daily?

[patient] Yes, I am.

[doctor] And have you missed any doses?

[patient] No, I haven't missed any.

[doctor] Alright. You also have diabetes type 2. How are you managing that?

[patient] I sometimes forget to take my metformin 500 mg twice daily.

[doctor] Hmm, okay. How high have your blood sugars been?

[patient] They've been ranging from 150 to 170 mg/dL.

[doctor] Alright. Do you have a sweet tooth?

[patient] Yes, I do. I love baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.

[doctor] That sounds delicious. How do you exercise?

[patient] I walk in the evening and in the morning. I walk about 1 to 2 miles per day around my neighborhood.

[doctor] Okay. Let's talk about your review of systems. How about your musculoskeletal system? Any other joint pain or issues?

[patient] No, just the right elbow pain.

[doctor] Alright. Let's check your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 120/70 mmHg, pulse rate is fine, respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute, oxygen saturation is 100% on room air, and your body temperature is 97.9 degrees F.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] I see that you've gained 5 pounds since your last visit. How has your diet been?

[patient] I've just been eating a lot of baked goods and sweet treats.

[doctor] Alright. Let's do a physical exam. I'm going to listen to your heart and lungs. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Your pulses are equal bilaterally. Your respiratory effort is normal, and your lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation. No rash or lesions on your skin. Let's examine your right elbow. There is moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. There is pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm, pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling, no discoloration or lesions.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] I also reviewed an x-ray of your right elbow today. It shows no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] Based on your symptoms and examination, it sounds like you have medial epicondylitis, which is an inflammation of the tendons on the inside of your elbow. This is likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in your arm.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] I'm going to recommend that we treat you conservatively. We will use a sling on your right arm while you're awake to provide increased support. I'll have you ice your right elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. You'll take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. We will also hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow your arm to heal.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] If you continue to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at your next visit.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Let's talk about your diabetes. I want you to check and record your blood sugars every day. I'm going to increase your metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. It's important to take your medication on a regular basis twice a day. I'll also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of your long-term blood sugar levels.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] Your hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. Continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. Keep up with your walking routine.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Do you have any questions for me?

[patient] No, I think that's it.

[doctor] Alright. We'll follow up with you in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss your progress. Do you have any questions?

[patient] No, I don't think so.

[doctor] Great. Take care, and I'll see you in 2 weeks.

[patient] Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor] You're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
