[patient] no, not really, but it's been going on for about a week now.
[doctor] okay, have you had any other symptoms like shortness of breath or dizziness?
[patient] no, i haven't had any of that.
[doctor] okay, and when you say chest pain, is it a sharp pain or a dull ache?
[patient] it's more of a dull ache.
[doctor] okay, and does it hurt when you push on your chest?
[patient] no, it only hurts when i'm doing something physically demanding.
[doctor] okay, alright, and how long have you had type 2 diabetes?
[patient] i've had that for about 10 years now.
[doctor] okay, and have you been doing well with it?
[patient] yeah, i've been doing okay.
[doctor] okay, and have you been taking your medications?
[patient] i've been taking my metformin, 500 milligrams twice a day.
[doctor] okay, and have you been taking it regularly?
[patient] yeah, i've been taking it every day.
[doctor] okay, and have you been checking your blood sugars?
[patient] i try to, but i haven't been doing it as much as i should.
[doctor] okay, alright. and how about your blood pressure? have you been monitoring that?
[patient] no, i haven't been monitoring it.
[doctor] okay, and have you had any problems with high blood pressure?
[patient] i haven't been formally diagnosed with hypertension, but my blood pressures have been high a lot.
[doctor] okay, and have you had any other medical problems?
[patient] i've had a family history of heart disease.
[doctor] okay, and have you had any other symptoms like nausea or vomiting?
[patient] no, i haven't had any of that.
[doctor] okay, and how about diarrhea?
[patient] no, i haven't had any of that either.
[doctor] okay, alright, so i'm just going to go ahead and do a quick physical exam on you. so i'm just going to listen to your heart and lungs now. so on your physical exam, your neck is normal, there's no jugular venous distention, no swelling, no carotid bruits. your lungs are clear bilaterally, no wheezes, no rales, no rhonchi. your heart has a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, otherwise normal rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops or rubs, and your pulses are equal. your abdomen is soft, no masses or tenderness, no guarding, and you have 1 plus nonpitting edema in your ankles, no calf tenderness. so, i'm going to go ahead and order an ekg, and we'll get that reviewed here in the office. i'm also going to order a hemoglobin a1c to check on your diabetes. so, looking at your blood work, your hemoglobin a1c is 7.0, which is good. so, for your diabetes, i think you're doing well on your current regimen, so i'm going to have you continue with metformin 500 milligrams twice daily, and i want you to keep your appointment with ophthalmology for an evaluation of diabetic retinopathy. i'm also going to put in an order for a repeat hemoglobin a1c to check for any needed medication adjustments, and i want you to follow up with me in two weeks. for your chest pain, i suspect that it might be unstable angina, considering your history of diabetes, your possible history of hypertension, and your family history of heart disease. your recent ekg was unremarkable, and there are no other signs of heart attack, but i'm worried that you might have some sort of blockage causing the pain. so, i'm going to have you start a regimen of baby aspirin at 81 milligrams daily, and i've sent in a prescription for that today. i also sent a prescription for nitroglycerin up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes as needed, and i want you to go to the emergency department if the medication is not effective after about 5 minutes. so, i'm also going to have you follow up with cardiology for further evaluation. and for your hypertension, i know several providers, including myself, have noted a trend of elevated blood pressures in your chart. so, i want you to monitor your blood pressures at home over the next two weeks and report your readings to me via mychart. this should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to your new medications. we discussed the possible side effects of hydrochlorothiazide, and how this will hopefully reduce some of the swelling that we see on your physical exam. all of your questions were answered, and you understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan. do you have any other questions for me?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] alright, i'm going to have my nurse come in and get you checked out. she'll be with you shortly to get you started on your medications and to get you scheduled for your cardiology follow-up. okay?
[patient] okay, sounds good. }}<|eot_id|>
