dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient] I'm doing okay, I've been having some knee pain.

[doctor] Hmm, okay. Can you tell me a little bit more about the knee pain? When did it start?

[patient] It started about a week ago.

[doctor] Okay. What was happening when you first felt the pain?

[patient] I was running around with my niece and I fell. I think I twisted my knee.

[doctor] I see. Did you hear a pop when you fell?

[patient] Yes, I heard a pop.

[doctor] And how would you describe the pain? Is it a throbbing pain?

[patient] Yes, it is.

[doctor] And how would you rate your pain level?

[patient] It's about a 4 out of 10.

[doctor] Okay. And how has your pain been since then?

[patient] It's been increasing.

[doctor] Okay. Have you been doing anything to treat the pain?

[patient] I've been taking Tylenol and wrapping my knee with kinesiology tape.

[doctor] Okay. And have you been able to walk on it?

[patient] I've been limping.

[doctor] Hmm. Have you taken any ibuprofen for the pain?

[patient] No, I can't take ibuprofen because I have an ulcer.

[doctor] Okay. Alright. Let me take a look at your knee. Can you lift up your pant leg for me?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] I'm going to press here. Does that hurt?

[patient] Yes, it does.

[doctor] Okay. Let me press here. How about this spot?

[patient] That hurts too.

[doctor] Okay. And how about this one?

[patient] That one hurts the most.

[doctor] Alright. Let me take a look at your knee. I'm going to move your knee around a little bit. Can you flex your knee for me?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] How about extending it?

[patient] It hurts when I do that too.

[doctor] Okay. Let's do a little bit of a special test called Lachman's test. Can you push your knee back and forth like this?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] And how about if I push it in this direction?

[patient] It doesn't hurt as much.

[doctor] Okay. And how about if I push it in this direction?

[patient] It hurts a little bit.

[doctor] Alright. Let's do another test called a valgus and varus stress test. Can you bend your knee and let me push your knee in this direction?

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] And how about if I push it in this direction?

[patient] It hurts a little bit.

[doctor] Okay. Let me take a look at the x-ray. Do you have any questions about what we've done so far?

[patient] No, I think you've covered everything.

[doctor] Alright. The x-ray shows no signs of a fracture or dislocation. It looks like you may have a strain of the MCL. We can treat you conservatively. I'm going to put you in a knee immobilizer to provide support for the next week. I'm also going to prescribe you some tramadol 50 mg to help with the pain. You can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. We'll also have you ice your knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. You should refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If your pain doesn't improve with the immobilizer, we may need to get an MRI.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good. Thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. Do you have any other questions?

[patient] No, I think that's it.

[doctor] Alright. We'll see you in 2 weeks.

[patient] Okay, thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
