dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi Emily, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing well, thanks. It's good to see you.

[doctor] Great to see you too, Emily. How has your asthma been lately?

[patient] It's been good. I've been managing it pretty well with my Wixela inhaler. I've actually been reducing my dose a bit.

[doctor] That's good to hear. How have you been reducing your dose?

[patient] I've been using it only once a day, three to four days a week. The other days, I only use it twice a day as prescribed.

[doctor] Okay, that sounds like you're trying to wean off it. How has that been working for you?

[patient] It's been working well. I don't feel like I need it as much as I used to.

[doctor] Good. That's a good sign. You mentioned you're trying to join the military. How's that going?

[patient] Yes, I'm trying to get enlisted. They require a certain fitness level without using an inhaler, so I'm trying to show them that I can do it without needing one.

[doctor] I see. You've been undergoing some exertional testing for that?

[patient] Yes, I'm doing that right now. They're giving me six to seven months to show that I can do it without needing any inhalers.

[doctor] Okay, that makes sense. How about your smoking and vaping habits?

[patient] I've been trying to quit, but I still smoke and vape. I've cut down a lot, though.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Cutting down is a big step. Have you had any recent respiratory issues or urgent care visits?

[patient] No, none at all. I haven't had any urgent care or emergency room visits for respiratory issues in the past year.

[doctor] Good. How about your nebulizer? Do you use it often?

[patient] No, I don't use it often. I only used it a few times as a child when I had an upper respiratory infection.

[doctor] Okay, that's good to know. How about your Zyrtec for your allergic rhinitis?

[patient] I still use it during the spring and fall. I take it three days a week when I work outside, and it works well.

[doctor] Great. Do you have any issues with tree nuts?

[patient] No, I avoid them. I had an incident last year where I was cleaning a rental car that had peanuts in it and I developed contact hives. But I've been careful and haven't had any other exposures since then.

[doctor] That's good to hear. You wear long sleeves and gloves when cleaning, right?

[patient] Yes, that's right. I always wear long sleeves and change my gloves between each cleaning job.

[doctor] Good, that's a good habit to have. Have you had any accidental exposures to peanuts recently?

[patient] No, none that I know of. I think it's possible that I've been in contact with cars that contained peanuts, but I haven't touched them directly.

[doctor] Alright, that's good. You always keep an EpiPen with you, right?

[patient] Yes, I always carry it with me.

[doctor] Good. Let's go over your current medications. You're on Wixela Inhub 250 mcg-50 mcg/dose powder for inhalation, 1 puff twice daily in the morning and evening. You also have Proair HFA as needed and an EpiPen as needed. Is that correct?

[patient] Yes, that's right.

[doctor] Okay. How about your Alvesco 80 mcg, 2 puffs, 2 times daily? Do you use that regularly?

[patient] Yes, I do. I use it as needed.

[doctor] Alright. Do you have any allergies?

[patient] Yes, I'm allergic to peanuts. I have a severe, anaphylactic reaction to them.

[doctor] Okay. And you also have an allergy to tree nuts, right?

[patient] Yes, I'm allergic to peanuts and tree nuts.

[doctor] You've been able to tolerate almonds and cashews, though?

[patient] Yes, I can tolerate them.

[doctor] That's good to know. Your Asthma Control Test score is 21, which is good. Your current food skin test sensitivities include peanuts.

[doctor] Let's review your plan. For your moderate persistent asthma, we'll start you on Alvesco, 2 puffs, twice daily. You can keep the Wixela in case Alvesco doesn't work well. You'll also continue to use albuterol as needed. Let's schedule a follow-up in four months to repeat the ACT and spirometry testing.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] For your allergic rhinitis, you'll continue to use Zyrtec 10 mg daily as needed.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] And for your anaphylactic reaction due to peanuts, you'll continue to avoid exposure. I'll refill your EpiPen today.

[patient] Thank you.

[doctor] Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that's all. Thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. We'll see you in four months for your follow-up. Take care.

[patient] Thank you, have a good day.

[doctor] You too. }}<|eot_id|>
