dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing okay, but I'm still having some issues with my irritable bowel syndrome.

[doctor] I see. Let's discuss your symptoms. How have you been feeling lately?

[patient] I've been having a lot of abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.

[doctor] Okay, I understand. You were last seen in August 2019, and we discussed your irritable bowel syndrome and functional abdominal pain. We started you on Bentyl, a low dose fiber supplement, probiotics, and instructed you to discontinue Reglan and use Zofran as needed for nausea. We also discussed starting neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar, but we decided not to start them yet.

[patient] Yes, that's right. I've been taking the Bentyl as prescribed.

[doctor] Good. How have you been managing your symptoms?

[patient] I've been having some flare-ups, but they're not as bad as they were before. I've been taking the Bentyl and the probiotics, and it seems to help a bit.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Can you tell me more about your symptoms? Have you noticed any specific triggers or patterns?

[patient] Well, I've been having less flare-ups from Monday to Thursday, but this week I'm having some cramps and sharp pains. I ate some shrimp yesterday and it caused upper and lower abdominal pain and bloating.

[doctor] Hmm, interesting. Have you noticed any other food triggers or patterns?

[patient] Yes, I get diarrhea after eating chicken alfredo, but I don't think that's related to my symptoms. I also drink 2 drinks when I'm out, either tequila or brown liquor, and occasionally a martini. I try to avoid heavy foods, but I had fried chicken today and didn't have any symptoms.

[doctor] Alright. It sounds like you've been trying to make some dietary changes. How about your soda intake? Have you cut down?

[patient] Yes, I've stopped drinking soda and that seems to have helped.

[doctor] That's good. How often do you go to the bathroom?

[patient] I usually go 2-3 times a day and it's all formed stools.

[doctor] Okay. I'll review your symptoms again. You're experiencing gastrointestinal symptoms like nausea, diarrhea, and abdominal pain. 

[patient] Yes, that's right.

[doctor] Let's do a quick physical exam. Your vital signs are all normal, your lungs are clear, and your heart rate and rhythm are regular. Your abdomen is soft and non-tender, and there are no masses or organomegaly. Your bowel sounds are normoactive.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Based on your symptoms and exam, I think you continue to have irritable bowel syndrome. We'll need to continue managing your symptoms with some lifestyle and dietary changes.

[patient] What do you recommend?

[doctor] I'm going to start you on a neuromodulator, Elavil 25 mg at bedtime, which may help with your IBS symptoms. You can also use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30-45 minutes before eating out at a restaurant. If you need to use Zofran for nausea, you can do so as well.

[patient] Okay, I'll try that.

[doctor] I also want you to avoid greasy, fatty foods and cut back on alcohol consumption.

[patient] Alright, I'll try to reduce those.

[doctor] Great. We'll need to monitor your response to the Elavil and see if any adjustments are needed. I'll send you a MyChart message in a month to check on your progress. If you need to come back sooner or have any side effects, please don't hesitate to reach out.

[patient] I'll do that. Thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. See you in a month, or sooner if needed. }}<|eot_id|>
