dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hello, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing okay. Just a bit concerned about my blood pressure.

[doctor] Ah, I understand. How high has it been?

[patient] It's been around 135-145 over 95-110.

[doctor] Hmm, that's a bit higher than we'd like. Do you check it regularly?

[patient] Yes, I do. I check it in the morning and evening.

[doctor] That's good. Do you notice any patterns? Is it higher at any particular time of day?

[patient] Yes, it's usually higher at night.

[doctor] Okay. And does it improve if you sit for a few minutes?

[patient] Yes, it does. It gets better after I sit for a bit.

[doctor] Alright. So we'll need to work on reducing your blood pressure. I'm going to prescribe some additional medication to help with that.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] I'm going to start you on naltrexone injections for your alcohol use disorder. Have you been drinking much lately?

[patient] Yeah, I drink about 4-5 times a week.

[doctor] I see. And are you interested in stopping or reducing your drinking?

[patient] Yes, I am. I know my blood pressure would improve if I stopped.

[doctor] That's great to hear. Naltrexone can help with that. We'll start the injections on May 17. Are you willing to give it a try?

[patient] Yes, I am.

[doctor] Good. We'll also continue the lisinopril 20 mg and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. It's important to take these medications regularly.

[patient] Okay, I'll keep taking them.

[doctor] How have you been feeling on the lisinopril?

[patient] Honestly, I've been experiencing some side effects like headaches, this weird sensation in my left ear, and feeling lethargic.

[doctor] Hmm, those are common side effects. Have you noticed any improvement when you took half the dose?

[patient] Yes, the side effects went away.

[doctor] That's good to know. We'll keep monitoring your blood pressure closely.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Do you have any other medical conditions?

[patient] No, just hypertension, hypercholesterolemia, and my alcohol use disorder.

[doctor] Alright. Any other symptoms or concerns?

[patient] No, nothing else.

[doctor] Good. Your physical exam today shows you appear to be in no distress, no fever, no tobacco or other substance use. Your neurological exam is normal, and you don't appear anxious, agitated, or depressed. Your neck veins are flat with your head at 90 degrees, your respiratory exam is clear, and your heart sounds are normal with no murmurs or gallop. You have no ankle edema and your skin is moist and good.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Based on your history and physical exam, I’m going to diagnose you with alcohol use disorder and hypertension. We’ll start the naltrexone injections and continue the lisinopril 10 mg and hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. If you can completely stop consuming alcohol, your blood pressure should improve significantly.

[patient] That sounds good.

[doctor] We’ll also upgrade your influenza vaccination and Shingrix #2. Do you have any questions?

[patient] No, I think I understand.

[doctor] Great. Make sure to call the clinic if you have any questions or concerns. Your next follow-up is scheduled for the week of June 14th, which will be the time of your second naltrexone injection. Do you have any other questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that's it.

[doctor] Alright. See you on June 14th.

[patient] Thanks, doctor.

[doctor] Take care, and see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
