dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hello, how are you today?

[patient] Hi, I'm okay, thanks. Just dealing with my rheumatoid arthritis.

[doctor] I see. I understand you're here for a follow-up today. How have you been managing your condition since your last visit?

[patient] It's been tough. The pain in my right knee has been getting worse. It's a 6 out of 10.

[doctor] I see. You mentioned in your note that the steroid injection we gave you last time only helped for a few weeks. Can you tell me more about that?

[patient] Yeah, it was helpful at first, but it wore off quickly. The pain and swelling are back.

[doctor] Okay, I understand. How has the pain been affecting your daily activities?

[patient] It's been making it hard to stand after sitting for a while. I have to use a cane to get my bearings.

[doctor] That sounds challenging. I see you're wearing support hose. How has that been helping?

[patient] It helps a bit, but the swelling in my right leg is still there.

[doctor] Alright, let's take a look at your knee. Can you tell me about any other symptoms you've noticed?

[patient] Yes, my whole right leg has been swollen since the last visit.

[doctor] Hmm, I see. I'll examine your knee. Your right knee feels swollen, and the skin is warm to the touch. I'll check for any pitting edema. Can you feel this pressure on your lower right leg?

[patient] Yeah, I can feel it.

[doctor] Good. Your range of motion is slightly reduced. Everything looks consistent with ongoing inflammation.

[patient] What does that mean?

[doctor] It means the inflammation is ongoing, which is not surprising given the pain and swelling you've described. We'll need to do some further testing to determine the best course of action.

[patient] What kind of testing?

[doctor] We'll do an X-ray of your right knee to see if there's any damage or joint space narrowing. We'll also refer you to physical therapy to help improve your range of motion and reduce the swelling.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] We'll also give you another steroid injection to help reduce the inflammation. I'll inject 80 mg of triamcinolone and 1 mL of lidocaine into your right knee. This should help with the pain and swelling.

[patient] Is that safe?

[doctor] Yes, it is. We'll discuss the potential risks, such as hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia. But we'll also go over the benefits. The injection should help reduce the inflammation and pain. Are you comfortable with that?

[patient] Yes, I am.

[doctor] Great. We'll also monitor your CMP levels regularly to ensure the Xeljanz you're taking is not causing any issues. It's a high-risk medication, and we need to keep an eye on that.

[patient] Okay, I understand.

[doctor] I'll also prescribe Celebrex 200 mg daily, which should help with pain and inflammation.

[patient] What about my diet and other things?

[doctor] I recommend you elevate your feet regularly, wear compression stockings, and follow a low-salt diet. These should help reduce the swelling. You should also check with your PCP for further instructions on managing the swelling.

[patient] Alright, I'll do that.

[doctor] We'll also arrange for an X-ray of your right knee and refer you to physical therapy. Let's get you scheduled for that.

[patient] Thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. We'll also follow up with your CMP levels every couple of months to monitor the Xeljanz. Do you have any other questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor] Great. We'll get everything set up and you'll be on your way. Take care and feel better soon.

[patient] Thank you, I appreciate it.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care, and we'll see you in a couple of months.

[patient] Okay, bye.

[doctor] Bye. }}<|eot_id|>
