dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient] Not too well, I've been having a lot of pain in my right shoulder.

[doctor] I see. Can you tell me more about what's been going on?

[patient] Sure. I was playing football a few days ago and got tackled a couple of times. I landed on my right shoulder and ever since then, I've had a lot of pain.

[doctor] Hmm, that sounds like it could be quite painful. Did you manage to continue playing after the injury?

[patient] Yeah, I did, but the pain has been there ever since.

[doctor] I see. Where exactly do you feel the pain?

[patient] It's mainly on the back of my shoulder.

[doctor] Okay. Do you feel any numbness or tingling in your fingers?

[patient] No, I don't feel any numbness or tingling in my fingers.

[doctor] Alright. How about the strength in your hand? Is that okay?

[patient] It's okay, but I've been trying to avoid lifting anything too heavy because of the pain.

[doctor] I understand. Can you touch your shoulder to your ear?

[patient] No, that's too painful.

[doctor] Okay. Have you been taking any pain medication?

[patient] Yes, I've been taking Tylenol, but it doesn't really help much.

[doctor] Hmm, I see. Do you have any neck problems or other joint problems?

[patient] No, I don't have any neck problems or other joint problems.

[doctor] Have you had any previous orthopedic injuries to your right shoulder?

[patient] No, I haven't had any previous injuries to my right shoulder.

[doctor] Okay. Have you had any fever or chills since the injury?

[patient] No, I haven't had any fever or chills.

[doctor] Do you have any headache?

[patient] Yes, I've had a headache.

[doctor] Hmm, okay. Did you hit your head at the time of the injury?

[patient] Yes, I hit my head when I fell, but I didn't pass out.

[doctor] Alright. Let's review your medical history. You had an appendectomy about 3 to 4 years ago, is that correct?

[patient] Yes, that's right.

[doctor] Okay. How about your overall health? Any fever or chills?

[patient] No, I haven't had any fever or chills.

[doctor] Any other symptoms?

[patient] Just the pain in my shoulder.

[doctor] Alright, let's check your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 122/76, your heart rate is 82 bpm, and your oxygen saturation is 95% on room air. Everything looks good there.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Now, let's do a physical exam. I'll start with your neck. Is there any tenderness to palpation?

[patient] No, my neck doesn't hurt.

[doctor] Okay. Let's move on to your head. Do you see any trauma or bruising?

[patient] No, there's no trauma or bruising.

[doctor] Alright. Now, let's examine your right shoulder. I'll check for any pain to palpation. Is there any pain on the back of your shoulder?

[patient] Yes, there is.

[doctor] Hmm. How about abduction and adduction of your right arm? Can you move it?

[patient] It's limited, I can only move it about 90 degrees.

[doctor] Okay. What about supination and pronation of your right arm? Is there any pain there?

[patient] Yes, there is pain.

[doctor] Alright. I can feel your radial artery pulse. It's normal. Now, let's do a quick X-ray of your right shoulder. The results show an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.

[patient] Oh no, what does that mean?

[doctor] It means you have a fracture in the joint between your shoulder blade and collarbone. We'll need to do a CT scan to evaluate it further.

[patient] Okay, what's the plan?

[doctor] We'll put you in a sling to help immobilize your shoulder and reduce the pain. I'll also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with pain. Additionally, I recommend you start formal physical therapy to strengthen your right shoulder. This will help you avoid surgery in the future.

[patient] Alright, that sounds good.

[doctor] Also, I advise you to avoid playing tackle football for now. It's best to give your shoulder time to heal.

[patient] Okay, I'll stop playing football.

[doctor] Great. We'll follow up with the CT scan and physical therapy. How do you feel about all of this?

[patient] I'm a bit worried, but I'll do what I need to do.

[doctor] That's a good attitude. We'll get you all fixed up and back on the road to recovery. Any questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think I understand.

[doctor] Alright, I'll see you in a few days for the CT scan and we'll go from there. Take care and see you soon.

[patient] Thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>
