[patient]: No, none of those.

[doctor]: Alright, let's review your medical history. You had a right total knee arthroplasty about two years ago, in the spring of 2020. How's your knee doing now?

[patient]: It's 95% better since the surgery.

[doctor]: That's great to hear. Let's check your vital signs. Your temperature is normal, blood pressure is 117/56 mm Hg, heart rate is 68 BPM, and oxygenation is 95% on room air. All good.

[doctor]: Now, I'll do a physical examination. I'll start with your neck. I'll check for any pain or tenderness. There is a bony protuberance at the C5-C6 area, but it doesn't hurt when I press on it.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Next, I'll examine your musculoskeletal system. I'll check your lumbar spine. There's no pain when I press on it, but you have decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. I'll also check for any muscle spasm. I do feel some spasm in the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Your strength is equal bilaterally.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: Now, I'll review the results of your x-rays. They didn't show any acute bony abnormalities.

[patient]: That's good to know.

[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the physical examination, it's possible that you may have an acute disc herniation. I'd like to get an MRI of your lumbar spine to confirm this.

[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor]: I'll prescribe you meloxicam 15 mg to help manage your pain. I'll also refer you to physical therapy to help with your back pain. Do you have any questions about this?

[patient]: No, that sounds good. Thank you.

[doctor]: Great. I'll get the MRI scheduled for you. We'll go over the results once we have them. Do you have any other questions?

[patient]: No, that's all for now.

[doctor]: Alright, I'll see you in a few weeks to follow up and discuss the MRI results. Take care and feel better.

[patient]: Thank you, I'll see you then. }}<|eot_id|>
