dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing okay, thanks. I'm here because of my knee.

[doctor] What's going on with your knee?

[patient] I hurt it playing soccer three days ago. I felt a pop and there was swelling.

[doctor] That sounds like a pretty significant injury. Can you tell me more about it?

[patient] Sure. I was playing a pickup game and I felt a pop in my right knee. There was swelling right away.

[doctor] Okay, did the swelling get worse or better over time?

[patient] It's gotten a bit better, but my knee isn't working as well as it used to.

[doctor] That must be frustrating. How would you rate your pain level right now?

[patient] It's about a 6 or 7 out of 10.

[doctor] And when the swelling goes down, how does that affect the pain?

[patient] It goes down to about a 4 or 5 out of 10.

[doctor] I see. Can you describe how your knee is moving?

[patient] I can bend it, but it hurts towards the end of the motion. When I try to straighten it, it feels like I have to help it straighten.

[doctor] Hmm, that doesn't sound good. Are you able to bear weight on your right knee?

[patient] Yes, but it feels loose and not normal.

[doctor] Can you tell me a bit about your work?

[patient] I work at a desk.

[doctor] Okay, so you have a desk job. Do you have any other symptoms besides the pain and swelling?

[patient] No, just the pain and stiffness.

[doctor] Alright, let's do a quick physical exam. Can you walk for me?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] I notice you're limping a bit on the right leg. That's concerning. Let me examine your knee. I see some swelling and a bit of ecchymosis. When I press on the joint, it hurts. It's difficult to move your knee passively. You're missing a few degrees of terminal extension and you're also having trouble bending it due to pain. You're missing about 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion because of the pain.

[patient] That sounds like a lot.

[doctor] Yes, it does. I'm also going to do a Lachman's test. This is a test for the ACL. It's positive. The strength in your leg muscles is normal, but the ACL is likely torn.

[patient] Oh no, that's not good.

[doctor] Let's discuss what we can do next. I think an MRI would be helpful to evaluate the ACL. This will give us a better idea of the extent of the damage.

[patient] Okay, that sounds like a good idea.

[doctor] Great. I'll prescribe you meloxicam 15 mg once a day to reduce the swelling and pain. It's important to use ice and modify your activities to reduce the swelling and pain.

[patient] Got it.

[doctor] I also recommend wearing a knee immobilizer and using crutches when you're walking.

[patient] Alright, I'll do that.

[doctor] You should avoid jogging until we get the results of the MRI.

[patient] Alright, I'll stop jogging.

[doctor] We'll follow up once we have the MRI results and discuss what we need to do next. Sound good?

[patient] Yes, that sounds good. Thanks for explaining everything.

[doctor] You're welcome. We'll get you scheduled for the MRI soon. Take care and see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
