dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm not so great. I've been having a lot of pain in my right elbow.

[doctor]: Oh, sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about the pain?

[patient]: It started four days ago, and it hasn't gotten any better. I've been using a pottery wheel to make a bowl for my son, and I think that might be the cause.

[doctor]: Ah, that sounds like it could be a good clue. Where exactly is the pain located?

[patient]: It's on the medial side of my elbow.

[doctor]: Does the pain radiate down your arm?

[patient]: Yes, it does. It goes down my arm, medially.

[doctor]: That can be quite uncomfortable. How would you rate your pain on a scale of 1 to 10?

[patient]: It's about a 7 at its worst.

[doctor]: Okay, got it. Does the pain wake you up at night?

[patient]: Yes, it does. If I roll over, it wakes me up.

[doctor]: Hmm, that can be quite bothersome. Have you tried using ibuprofen or applying ice to see if it helps?

[patient]: No, I haven't tried either of those.

[doctor]: Alright, we'll try those next. Have you had any previous elbow injuries?

[patient]: No, none that I know of.

[doctor]: Okay, good. Do you have any other medical conditions?

[patient]: Yes, I have hypertension and diabetes type 2.

[doctor]: How have you been managing those?

[patient]: I take lisinopril 10 mg daily and metformin 500 mg twice daily. I usually check my blood pressure regularly, and it's been in the 110s to 130s range.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. How often do you forget to take your metformin?

[patient]: Sometimes I forget to take it, especially when I'm busy.

[doctor]: I see. How do you manage your blood sugar levels?

[patient]: They usually range from 150 to 170 mg/dL.

[doctor]: That's important to keep an eye on. What kind of exercise do you do?

[patient]: I walk 1 to 2 miles per day around my neighborhood in the morning and evening.

[doctor]: Good exercise routine. Do you have any hobbies?

[patient]: Yes, I enjoy working with ceramics and baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.

[doctor]: That sounds like fun. Let me review your medical history. You have hypertension and diabetes type 2.

[patient]: Yes, that's right.

[doctor]: You're currently taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.

[patient]: Yes, that's correct.

[doctor]: Let's go over your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 120/70 mmHg, your pulse rate is appropriate, your respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute, your oxygen saturation is 100% on room air, and your body temperature is 97.9 degrees Fahrenheit. You've gained 5 pounds since your last visit.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Now, let's do a physical exam. I'll check your cardiovascular system, respiratory system, and skin. I'll also examine your musculoskeletal system. Your heart sounds are normal, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Your pulses are equal bilaterally. Your lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation. Your skin is normal, no rash or lesions. 

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Now, let's examine your right elbow. There is moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. You experience pain when I resist pronation of your right forearm and when you flex and bend your right wrist. There's no bruising or swelling, no discoloration or lesions.

[patient]: That hurts a bit.

[doctor]: Alright, I'm going to order an x-ray of your right elbow. Let me review the results. The x-ray shows no evidence of any fractures, and there are no other abnormalities noted.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the exam, it appears that you have medial epicondylitis, which is inflammation of the tendons in your arm. I think this was caused by overuse and damage from using the pottery wheel.

[patient]: Oh, I see.

[doctor]: I'm going to recommend a conservative treatment approach. You'll need to wear a sling on your right arm while you're awake to provide support. You'll also apply ice to your right elbow for 20 minutes, three times a day. Take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for one week. It's also important that you avoid using the pottery wheel for the next two weeks to allow your arm to heal.

[patient]: Okay, I'll do that.

[doctor]: I've also increased your metformin to 1000 mg twice a day to help with your diabetes. It's crucial that you take your medication regularly, twice a day. We'll also schedule a hemoglobin A1c test to get a more accurate assessment of your blood sugar levels.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: Your hypertension is currently under control with your current medication and exercise. Continue taking lisinopril 10 mg and keep up with your walking routine.

[patient]: Okay, I'll keep doing that.

[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think you've covered everything.

[doctor]: Great. We'll schedule a follow-up in two weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss your progress. Do you have any other questions?

[patient]: No, that's all.

[doctor]: Alright, I'll see you in two weeks. Take care and get well soon.

[patient]: Thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>
