dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you today?

[patient] Good morning, doctor. I'm doing okay, thanks. I've been having some issues with my knee lately.

[doctor] Ah, I'm glad you're here. What seems to be the problem with your knee?

[patient] Well, about two and a half weeks ago, I stepped off a curb and twisted my ankle. Since then, I've been having a lot of pain on the side of my right knee.

[doctor] Hmm, I see. Can you show me exactly where the pain is?

[patient] Yes, it's right here. On the lateral side.

[doctor] Okay. Do you notice any other symptoms, like clicking or anything else?

[patient] Yes, when I walk, I can hear a clicking sound. But it doesn't hurt when I'm walking unless I stand on one foot for a long time.

[doctor] Got it. And how about when you stand for a long time, do you feel any pain then?

[patient] Yes, my knee locks up when I'm standing for a while. And if I rest my foot on my heel, I feel pain.

[doctor] Okay. How about when you're at rest or when your foot is flat on the ground, is there any pain then?

[patient] No, there isn't.

[doctor] Alright. How has your activity level been? Has it affected your daily activities?

[patient] I'm actually more active during the day, but it doesn't seem to affect the pain.

[doctor] That's good to know. Can you do a full squat without pain?

[patient] Yes, I can do a full squat without any pain.

[doctor] And do you have any numbness or tingling in your right foot?

[patient] Yes, I have numbness and tingling in my right foot, but it's not new. It's been there since the ankle injury.

[doctor] Alright. Have you been trying any treatments for the pain?

[patient] Yes, I've been taking ibuprofen at night, and it helps with the pain. I've also been icing my knee four times a day, which helps in the morning and at the end of the day.

[doctor] Okay. Let's go over your medical history. Have you had any other injuries or illnesses?

[patient] I've fractured my second toe three times over the last 10 years.

[doctor] I see. Do you work?

[patient] Yes, I work in sales.

[doctor] And do you have any exercise habits?

[patient] I ride a bike for exercise.

[doctor] Alright. Let's do a quick review of your systems. How's your neurological system been?

[patient] I have numbness and tingling in my toes on my right foot from the ankle injury.

[doctor] Okay. Any other issues?

[patient] No, nothing else.

[doctor] Let's do a physical exam. I'll check your cardiovascular system first. Do you have any issues with your heart or blood circulation?

[patient] No, none that I know of.

[doctor] Okay. Now, I'll check your musculoskeletal system. I'll start with your right knee. I'll check for any swelling or fluid accumulation. I'll also check for pain when I press on different areas. I'll also perform some special tests like the varus test.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Let's start with the examination. There's some slight edema and a slight effusion in your right knee. There's no pain when I press on the lateral aspect. The varus test is positive, indicating a possible ligament strain. Your range of motion is full, and the Lachman's test is negative.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Now, I'll order some x-rays of your right knee to rule out any fractures or bony abnormalities.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] The results of the x-rays show no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormalities. Based on the examination and the x-ray results, I suspect a right knee lateral collateral ligament strain.

[patient] A ligament strain?

[doctor] Yes, it's a strain in the ligament on the outside of your knee. We'll treat it conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen your right knee. I'll also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg to help with the pain. If your pain doesn't improve with physical therapy, we'll consider getting an MRI.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good. What's the plan for the physical therapy?

[doctor] We'll schedule an appointment with our physical therapist, who will work with you to develop a personalized exercise plan to strengthen your knee. We'll also monitor your progress and adjust the plan as needed.

[patient] Alright, that sounds good. When do I start the physical therapy?

[doctor] We'll get you scheduled for an appointment within the next week. We'll also start you on the meloxicam today. Take one tablet once a day with food. If you have any questions or concerns, feel free to reach out to us.

[patient] Alright, thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. We'll see how you progress with the physical therapy and adjust the treatment plan accordingly. Take care.

[patient] Thank you, doctor. I'll see you soon.

[doctor] See you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
