dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hello, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm okay, but I've been having some issues with my foot.

[doctor] Oh, I'm sorry to hear that. What seems to be the problem?

[patient] My right foot has an open sore that won't heal.

[doctor] I see. How long has this sore been there?

[patient] It started about a year ago. I think it was just a blister at first, but I picked at it and it turned red.

[doctor] Okay, and what happened after that?

[patient] My primary care doctor prescribed me Keflex and it seemed to help, but it didn't really clear up.

[doctor] Hmm, that's interesting. When did you stop taking the Keflex?

[patient] About 5 months ago.

[doctor] Alright. Have you noticed any pain with the sore?

[patient] No, but I do feel less sensation in my foot.

[doctor] That's concerning. Have you noticed any other symptoms?

[patient] Yes, there's a lot of drainage and I feel really tired all the time.

[doctor] Okay, I see. Have you been wearing your regular shoes?

[patient] Yes, I haven't changed my routine.

[doctor] Alright, let me go over your medical history. You have type 2 diabetes with neuropathy, correct?

[patient] Yes, that's right.

[doctor] How have your blood sugars been lately?

[patient] They're better than they used to be, but they're still around 200.

[doctor] And what was your last hemoglobin A1C?

[patient] It was 10.

[doctor] Okay, let's go over some other systems. How have you been feeling overall?

[patient] I'm tired all the time.

[doctor] And how about your skin? Any other issues besides the sore on your foot?

[patient] No, just the foot.

[doctor] Alright, let me check your vitals. No fever, and your vitals look good today.

[patient] Good.

[doctor] Now, let's take a look at your foot. There is a 1 cm x 2 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. I see some yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. There's no surrounding erythema or cellulitis, and no evidence of fluid collection. I don't see any necrosis, and there's no odor. I also don't see any bony exposure. No pain to palpation.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Let me order an ankle brachial index to check the blood supply to your foot. This will help us determine if we can heal the wound. We'll also do a debridement in the office today. 

[patient] Alright, that sounds good.

[doctor] I'll also recommend a surgical shoe to provide increased support. Additionally, I'm going to prescribe collagenase ointment for you to apply to the wound once a day, and cover it with a sterile dressing. This should be continued until we see the ulcer decrease in size.

[patient] Okay, I'll do that.

[doctor] Great. We'll follow up with you in 2 weeks to check on your progress.

[patient] Alright, thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care, and we'll see you in 2 weeks.

[patient] Thank you, I'll see you then. }}<|eot_id|>
