[patient] No, everything sounds normal.

[doctor] Good. Now, let's check your musculoskeletal system. Can you move your right arm for me?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] Can you flex and extend your elbow? Any pain or discomfort?

[patient] Yes, there's pain when I do that.

[doctor] And when I press on the back of your elbow, does that hurt?

[patient] Yes, that hurts too.

[doctor] I see. There's some swelling and ecchymosis around your elbow as well. Let me take a look at it. There's a bruise on your elbow. It looks like an injury. We'll do an X-ray to rule out any fractures or other bony abnormalities.

[patient] Okay, sounds good.

[doctor] We'll go ahead and order an X-ray of your right elbow. The results show no evidence of fracture or bony abnormalities. Based on your symptoms and the X-ray, it appears that you have a right elbow contusion.

[patient] A contusion? What does that mean?

[doctor] It means you have a bruise or injury to the soft tissues around your elbow. It's likely caused by the lacrosse stick hitting your elbow. The pain and swelling are common symptoms.

[patient] Oh, I see. What do I need to do?

[doctor] I'll prescribe ibuprofen 800 mg twice a day to help with the pain and swelling. You should also ice the area in 20-minute intervals three times a day to reduce the swelling and pain. Avoid playing lacrosse for the next couple of weeks until your elbow heals.

[patient] Alright, that sounds good. What about my blood pressure? Is that related to the elbow?

[doctor] No, your blood pressure is a separate issue. You've been well-controlled with dietary modifications and lisinopril. Keep up with those dietary modifications and you should be fine.

[patient] Okay, got it. And how about my diabetes?

[doctor] Your diabetes appears to be well-controlled at this time. We'll keep you on metformin 500 mg. We'll also order a hemoglobin A1c to evaluate if any medication adjustments are needed.

[patient] Alright, I understand. What else should I do?

[doctor] Just follow the treatment plan I mentioned, including the ibuprofen and icing the elbow. If the pain gets worse or you notice any other symptoms, come back to see me. Otherwise, we'll follow up in a few weeks to check on your progress.

[patient] Okay, I'll do that. Thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care and feel better soon.

[patient] Thanks, I will. Bye.

[doctor] Bye. }}<|eot_id|>
