dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hello, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm okay. Thanks for seeing me.

[doctor] Of course, I'm Dr. Smith. What brings you in today?

[patient] I've been having some pain and swelling in my right ankle.

[doctor] Hmm, okay. Can you tell me more about when the pain started?

[patient] It started yesterday. I slipped on the ice while taking out the trash and fell. I couldn't move at first, and a friend had to help me up.

[doctor] Oh no, that sounds like it must have been quite painful. How did you manage your ankle after the fall?

[patient] I iced it and elevated it throughout the night. The pain has improved a bit, but I'm still limping.

[doctor] I see. Have you noticed any numbness in your right foot?

[patient] No, I haven't.

[doctor] Good. Have you had any other injuries to your right ankle in the past?

[patient] No, this is the first time I've ever had any issues with my right ankle.

[doctor] Alright. How about your activity level? Do you do anything that might be related to this injury?

[patient] Yes, I'm very active. I play soccer in the community league.

[doctor] Okay, that's good to know. Let's go over your medical history now. Have you noticed any other symptoms besides the pain and swelling?

[patient] No, just the pain and swelling.

[doctor] Alright. Any other medical conditions you're currently managing?

[patient] No, I'm generally healthy.

[doctor] Great. Let's move on to your vital signs. Your blood pressure, heart rate, and oxygen saturation all look good today.

[patient] Okay, that's good to hear.

[doctor] Now, let's examine your ankle. I'm going to gently feel your ankle to see if there's any tenderness or swelling. There is some mild edema present. How about any numbness or tingling?

[patient] No, I don't feel any numbness.

[doctor] Alright. I'll also check your ankle's range of motion. You mentioned you can bear more weight on your right ankle, but you're still limping?

[patient] Yes, that's right.

[doctor] Okay. I'll now perform some special tests to check for any instability. There's no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness when I palpate your foot. Your neurovascular exam is intact. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds, and your dorsalis pedis pulse is strong.

[patient] Alright, that sounds good.

[doctor] I also took three views of your ankle. The x-rays didn't show any signs of a fracture.

[patient] That's a relief.

[doctor] Based on your symptoms and the exam findings, it appears that you've sustained a right ankle sprain. We'll provide an Aircast to help stabilize your ankle.

[patient] Okay, that sounds like it will help.

[doctor] Yes, it will. You should keep your right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. Continue to ice your ankle. You'll also need to use crutches for the next 1 to 2 days and then progress to weight-bearing as tolerated.

[patient] Alright, I'll do that.

[doctor] For pain control, you can continue to take NSAIDs as needed.

[patient] Okay, I'll keep that in mind.

[doctor] We'll also schedule a follow-up appointment in 2 weeks to assess your progress and determine your return to play date.

[patient] That sounds good. I'll make sure to come back.

[doctor] Great. If your symptoms change or worsen before then, please don't hesitate to call the office.

[patient] I'll do that. Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care and see you in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
