[patient]: Yes, the pain I mentioned earlier.

[doctor]: Any shortness of breath?

[patient]: No, I don't feel short of breath.

[patient]: No nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea?

[patient]: No, none of those.

[doctor]: Alright. Let's do a physical exam. How's your neck feeling?

[patient]: It's fine.

[doctor]: No swelling, JVD, or carotid bruits?

[patient]: No.

[doctor]: How's your respiratory system?

[patient]: My breathing is fine.

[doctor]: Any wheezes, rales, or rhonchi?

[patient]: No.

[doctor]: Let's listen to your heart. Any murmurs, gallops, or rubs?

[patient]: No.

[doctor]: Your heart rate and rhythm seem normal. How about your abdomen?

[patient]: It feels fine.

[doctor]: Any masses or tenderness?

[patient]: No.

[doctor]: Your abdomen feels soft and there's no guarding. How about your ankles?

[patient]: They feel a bit swollen.

[doctor]: Any calf tenderness?

[patient]: No.

[doctor]: Alright, let's take a look at your EKG. It looks unremarkable. Your hemoglobin A1c is 7.0.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Based on your history of diabetes, possible history of hypertension, and family history of heart disease, I suspect your chest pain might be unstable angina. I'm worried there might be some sort of blockage causing your pain.

[patient]: Oh, I'm worried too.

[doctor]: We discussed proper protocol for sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain. If it's not effective after 5 minutes, you should go to the emergency department.

[patient]: Okay, I'll do that.

[doctor]: I'm going to start you on a regimen of baby aspirin at 81 mg daily. I've sent in a prescription for you. Also, you'll have nitroglycerin up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes as needed. I've sent a prescription for that as well.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: We'll have you follow up with cardiology for further evaluation.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: How about your diabetes? How's that been going?

[patient]: It seems like it's under control.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. We'll have you follow up in 2 weeks to check your hemoglobin A1c to see if any medication adjustments are needed. You'll continue with metformin 500 mg twice daily and dietary modifications.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: I've also noticed that your blood pressures have been trending high. We'll have you monitor your blood pressures at home over the next 2 weeks and report them to me via MyChart. This will help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to your new medications. We'll also start you on hydrochlorothiazide at a low dose.

[patient]: Alright, I'll do that.

[doctor]: Do you have any questions about any of this?

[patient]: No, I think I understand.

[doctor]: Great. You'll follow up in 2 weeks. Do you have any other questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think that's all.

[doctor]: Alright, I'll see you in 2 weeks. Take care and don't hesitate to reach out if you have any issues.

[patient]: Thank you, doctor. I'll see you then. }}<|eot_id|>
