dialogue is: {{ [doctor] gregory hernandez is a 42-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right ankle pain and swelling. his onset of pain began yesterday after he slipped and fell on the ice while taking out the trash. he states that he was unable to ambulate at the time of injury and that a friend had to help him up. he then proceeded to ice and elevate his ankle throughout the night. his pain has since improved and he is still limping. he denies experiencing any numbness to his right foot. in addition to ice and elevation he has been taking ibuprofen for his pain, which has provided some relief. he does not recall any other previous injury to his right ankle. any questions for me?
[patient] no
[doctor] alright. so, gregory, i'm gon na go ahead and review your vitals. your vitals look good today. i'm gon na go ahead and do a quick physical exam. and i'm gon na tell you what i find. so, i'm gon na start with your cardiovascular exam. your heart is in a regular rate and rhythm. i'm gon na go ahead and do a quick neurovascular exam. your neurovascular exam is normal. i'm gon na go ahead and do a musculoskeletal exam. so, your right ankle examination reveals ecchymosis. there is swelling over the lateral malleolus. there is tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. no bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. neurovascular intact. capillary refill is less than three seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse. sensation is intact to light touch. i'm gon na go ahead and review the results of your x-rays. so, your x-rays of the right ankle do not reveal any evidence of fracture. after reviewing your examination and radiographic findings today, i had a lengthy discussion with you regarding your current symptoms. we discussed that your x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture and that you sustained a right ankle sprain. treatment options were discussed. i advised you to keep your right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. you will also continue to ice your right ankle. an aircast was provided today to help stabilize your ankle. you are to also utilize crutches for the next one to two days followed by weight-bearing as tolerated. for pain control, you can continue to take nsaids as needed. we discussed that you should see significant improvement in your symptoms over the next two weeks. any questions for me?
[patient] no
[doctor] alright. so, gregory, i'm gon na have you follow up with me in two weeks to assess your progress and to determine your return to play date. you are advised to call the office should your symptoms change or worsen prior to your next appointment. is that okay?
[patient] okay
[doctor] alright. that's all for today. thank you, gregory. }}<|eot_id|>
