dialogue is: {{ [doctor] angela powell is a pleasant 81-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. the onset of her pain began one week ago, when she was running around with her niece and fell. at the time of the injury, she heard a pop and believes that she twisted her knee. she reports that she has been limping since the injury. the patient locates her pain to the medial and lateral aspects of her knee. she rates her pain level as a four out of ten, however this is increasing. her pain is described as a throbbing pain. the patient has been taking tylenol, which has provided mild and temporary relief, as well as wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. she states that she is unable to take ibuprofen secondary to an ulcer.
[doctor] okay. so, you were running around with your niece and you fell, and you heard a pop. did you hear it pop when you were running or when you were getting up from running?
[patient] when i was running.
[doctor] okay. and then you fell. did you fall on your left leg or your right leg?
[patient] left.
[doctor] okay. and you heard a pop. did you feel it on the inside or outside of your knee?
[patient] outside.
[doctor] okay. and then you've been limping since then. have you been able to walk on it at all?
[patient] yeah.
[doctor] okay. and you've been limping, and you've been taking tylenol, and wrapping your knee with kinesiology tape. and you're not able to take ibuprofen because of an ulcer. is that correct?
[patient] yeah.
[doctor] okay. and you've been having pain on the inside and outside of your knee. is that correct?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. and the pain is throbbing, and it's getting worse. is that correct?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. have you ever hurt your knee before?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical problems?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and are you on any medications?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any surgeries?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. all right. let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. on physical exam of your left knee, there is some edema and effusion. moderate range of motion. pain with flexion and extension of the knee. negative lachman's. negative valgus and varus stress test.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right. let's go ahead and take a look at your x-rays. four views of your left knee were taken. these reveal no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. no other abnormalities are noted.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right. so, based on your exam and your radiographic findings, it looks like you have a possible mcl strain. i'm gon na go ahead and put you in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week. i'm gon na prescribe you tramadol 50 milligrams, and you can take tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. i'm gon na have you ice your left knee three to four times a day for twenty to thirty minutes at a time. and i'm gon na have you refrain from running for the next three weeks. if your pain does not improve with the immobilizer, i'm gon na have you obtain an mri.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] does that sound good?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] all right. is there anything else i can help you with today?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. i'm gon na go ahead and go ahead and get my nurse to help you get you into the immobilizer. do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. all right. i'll see you in three weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
